--------------------------------------------- Live race -------------------------------------------

- DISPLAY THE LIVE RACE FROM FAKE POSITIONS EMITTED BY A HAND-MADE RXJS OBSERVABLE.

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite "Advanced observables" chapter

- We would now like to create a "live" page to see the ponies when they are racing


1️⃣ First, let’s add an input to our Pony to reflect if the pony is running or not, so we can
    display a nice running pony during the race. To do this, 👉you’ll add an input field called
    isRunning (false by default). You’ll also need to 👉modify the ponyImageUrl computed field to
    make it return a URL like images/pony-blue-running.gif if the pony’s color is blue and the
    flag isRunning is true. When that’s done, add the images into the public/images folder.
    Pony.ts:

        readonly isRunning = input(false);
        protected readonly ponyImageUrl = computed(() => {
            return this.isRunning()
            ? `images/pony-${this.ponyModel().color.toLowerCase()}-running.gif`
            : `images/pony-${this.ponyModel().color.toLowerCase()}.gif`
        })
    
    or the 🔥shorthand and more efficient way: 

        readonly isRunning = input(false);
        protected readonly ponyImageUrl = computed(
            () => `images/pony-${this.ponyModel().color.toLowerCase()}${this.isRunning() 
            ? '-running' 
            : ''}.gif`
        );


2️⃣ Let’s now focus on the "live" page. New page = New component!
    Create a component live. 👉Add the necessary configuration in the router to be able to navigate
    to this component using the URL /races/:raceId/live (as a new child of the 'races' path), and
    👉add a "Watch live!" link in the Bet page, with the correct parameters.
    app.routes.ts:

        {
            path: 'races',
            children: [
                { path: '', component: Races },
                { path: ':raceId', component: Bet },
                { path: ':raceId/live', component: Live },
            ]
        }
    
    bet.html:

        <a [routerLink]="['/races', race.id, 'live']" class="btn btn-primary">Watch live!</a>


3️⃣ Now we need to receive the positions of the ponies during the race. 💡The backend exposes a
    websocket channel for this, 💡but we are going to work without it first, by emulating these
    positions using RxJS.
    👉To represent this position, add another interface called PonyWithPositionModel in pony-model.ts.
    👉This new interface will extend PonyModel and have a field position of type number. Indeed, the
    server will emit positions as integers for each pony between 0 (the pony has not started) and
    100 (the pony has completed the race), until one of the ponies reaches 100.
    pony-model.ts:

        export interface PonyModel {
            // omitted
        }
        export interface PonyWithPositionModel extends PonyModel {
            position: number;
        }

    👉Create also a LiveRaceModel interface in race-model.ts, containing a field ponies as an array
    of PonyWithPositionModel and a status field.
    👉This status field is an enum on server-side, with 3 possible values:
    -> 'PENDING' if the race is not started yet
    -> 'RUNNING' if it’s currently started
    -> 'FINISHED' if we missed the action
    👉Let’s add an optional status? field in our RaceModel, and a mandatory status field in our
    LiveRaceModel with these values.
    race-model.ts:

        export interface RaceModel {
            // omitted
            status?: 'PENDING' | 'RUNNING' | 'FINISHED';
        }

        export interface LiveRaceModel {
            ponies: Array<PonyWithPositionModel>;
            status?: 'PENDING' | 'RUNNING' | 'FINISHED';
        }


4️⃣ Now 👉add a method in the RaceService called live that will receive the race’s id, and that will
    return an Observable emitting a LiveRaceModel every second.

        live(raceId: number): Observable<LiveRaceModel> {
            // to be implemented
        }

    🔥In the method, use an Observable to emit positions every second. To emit data at regular
    intervals, RxJS offers the interval function.

        const positions = interval(200);
        positions.subscribe(position => console.log(position));
        // logs 0, 1, 2, 3, 4...
    
    ✅This will emit a sequence of integer starting from 0 to Infinity, every 200ms. That’s a good
    start. Adapt it slightly to emit an integer every second.
    👉We don’t want to emit numbers. We want to emit a live race with an array of positions every
    second. So add a map operator to the chain, transforming the position received into an object
    like this one:

        {
            ponies: [
                {
                    id: 1,
                    name: 'Superb Runner',
                    color: 'BLUE',
                    position
                },
                {
                    id: 2,
                    name: 'Awesome Fridge',
                    color: 'GREEN',
                    position
                },
                {
                    id: 3,
                    name: 'Great Bottle',
                    color: 'ORANGE',
                    position
                },
                {
                    id: 4,
                    name: 'Little Flower',
                    color: 'YELLOW',
                    position
                },
                {
                    id: 5,
                    name: 'Nice Rock',
                    color: 'PURPLE',
                    position
                }
            ],
            status: 'RUNNING'
        }
    
    💡As you see the position of each pony is determined by the position received in the map.
    That means all the ponies will go at the same pace, running one percent of the race every second.
    ⚠️In reality that will never be the case (the ponies don’t have the same pace), but it will be
    very handy for us to start coding the "live" component with these positions.
    💡We only need the ponies to go to the position 100, so that means we need to stop after the
    101th element emitted. As always with RxJS, there is an operator for that: take(n).
    for example:

        const positions = interval(200).pipe(
            take(2)
        );
        positions.subscribe(position => console.log(position));
        // logs only 0 and 1 and stops

    👉Add the take operator to the chain to stop emitting value after the 101th one.
    race-service.ts:

        live(raceId: number): Observable<LiveRaceModel> {
            return 👉interval(1000).pipe(
            take(101), 👈
          👉map(position => ({
                ponies: [
                {
                    id: 1,
                    name: 'Superb Runner',
                    color: 'BLUE',
                    position
                },
                {
                    id: 2,
                    name: 'Awesome Fridge',
                    color: 'GREEN',
                    position
                },
                {
                    id: 3,
                    name: 'Great Bottle',
                    color: 'ORANGE',
                    position
                },
                {
                    id: 4,
                    name: 'Little Flower',
                    color: 'YELLOW',
                    position
                },
                {
                    id: 5,
                    name: 'Nice Rock',
                    color: 'PURPLE',
                    position
                }
                ],
                status: 'RUNNING'
            }))
            );
        }
    
    We should now have everything we need in the service: a method returning an observable, which
    is emitting a live race with an array of positions every second.


5️⃣ Let’s go back to our Live component. We want to:
    👉first, fetch the race details (to get the name, the start instant, the initial status, etc…​)
    Create a required input named raceId, transformed with numberAttribute, so the router can
    bind the dynamic URL parameter to it. create a first resource field to store the result of
    RaceService.get().       
    live.ts:

        private readonly raceService = inject(RaceService);
        readonly raceId = input.required({ transform: numberAttribute });
        protected readonly initialRace = this.raceService.get(() => this.raceId());

    👉then, fetch the live information with the updated status and the positions of the ponies.
    We’ll store these information in an object described by the following interface (that you
    can add in the live.ts file):

        interface RaceModelWithPositions extends RaceModel {
            poniesWithPosition: Array<PonyWithPositionModel>;
        }
    
    (As you can see RaceModelWithPositions is the same as the RaceModel interface, but with an
    additional field poniesWithPosition, representing the ponies with their positions.)
    👉Then add a new resource, that depends on the initial race resource and call RaceService.live()
    only once the race has been loaded. You can use rxResource() for that:
    live.ts:

        protected readonly initialRace = this.raceService.get(() => this.raceId());
        private readonly liveRace = rxResource<RaceModelWithPositions, RaceModel | undefined>({
            params: () => {
                if (!this.initialRace.hasValue()) {
                    return undefined;
                }
                return this.initialRace.value();
            },
            stream: ({ params: race }) =>
                this.raceService.live(race.id).pipe(
                    map(live => ({ ...race, poniesWithPosition: live.ponies, status: live.status })),
                    startWith({ ...race, poniesWithPosition: [] })
                )
        });

    👉Add a computed() field named raceModel that returns undefined while the initial race is not
    loaded, or the value of the live resource once available:
    live.ts:

        protected readonly raceModel = computed<RaceModelWithPositions | undefined>(() => {
            if (!this.initialRace.hasValue()) {
                return undefined;
            }
            const race = this.initialRace.value();
            return this.liveRace.hasValue() ? this.liveRace.value() : { ...race, poniesWithPosition: [] };
        });



6️⃣ Then, 👉use the raceModel field in an h1 element to display the name of the race in the template.
    The last thing to do is to display our moving ponies. In the template 👉add a div with a class
    pony-wrapper, which will be repeated for each pony in the poniesWithPosition array.
    👉Inside this div, add a Pony for the current pony, with an attribute isRunning set to true
    (to make the pony run, of course. (property binding)).

    👉We now need to "move" our ponies based on their positions. To do this, set the 'margin-left'
    style property to (pony.position - 5)%, by adding [style.marginLeft.%]="pony.position - 5" to
    the div wrapping the pony.
    live.html:

        @if (raceModel(); as raceModel) {
            <h1>{{ raceModel.name }}</h1>
            @for (pony of raceModel.poniesWithPosition; track pony.id) {
                <div class="pony-wrapper" [style.marginLeft.%]="pony.position - 5" style="transition: margin-left 1s linear">
                <pr-pony [ponyModel]="pony" [isRunning]="true" />
                </div>
            }
        }


